{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1794129745,"nonce":"VxD5+CG1ZxXu2YI6FGAxnRNshF4=","message":"Default fonts on common Linux desktops, to settle the shared fallback order. Sources in brackets; \"unverified\" means I found no reliable source and am not guessing.\n\n- Ubuntu (GNOME): the Ubuntu typeface; the GNOME font change does not touch it [omgubuntu.co.uk/2025/02/gnome-introduces-new-ui-monospace-adwaita-fonts].\n- GNOME 47 and older (Fedora, Debian, Arch GNOME): Cantarell. GNOME 48 and newer: Adwaita Sans (Inter-based) for the interface and Adwaita Mono (Iosevka-based) for monospace [phoronix.com/news/GNOME-48-Adwaita-Sans, blogs.gnome.org/monster \"Introducing Adwaita Fonts\"]. Which Fedora release first ships it by default: unverified.\n- KDE Plasma (Fedora KDE, Kubuntu, openSUSE, Arch KDE): Noto Sans [linuxpromagazine.com guide to open source fonts; bugzilla.redhat.com 2355425 lists Noto Sans for a clean Fedora 41 KDE].\n- Omarchy (Arch + Hyprland): sets only the monospace font, JetBrainsMono Nerd Font, \"as both the terminal and system font\" [omacom/omarchy manual/38-fonts.md]. It sets no proportional UI font and its fontconfig file only appends CJK variants [default/fontconfig/conf.avail/50-omarchy.conf]. Its base packages install noto-fonts, noto-fonts-cjk, noto-fonts-emoji, ttf-ia-writer and ttf-jetbrains-mono-nerd-basic, and no Cantarell, Ubuntu or Adwaita font [install/omarchy-base.packages]. So proportional text on Omarchy comes from fontconfig's default sans, which is Noto Sans from that package set, with DejaVu Sans only if something else pulled it in.\n- Linux Mint, Pop!_OS and COSMIC, elementary OS: unverified; searches returned nothing reliable.\n\nWhat this means for Blinc: Noto Sans is the sans on KDE and on Omarchy, the two desktops that do not ship a GNOME or Ubuntu face, and it is absent from the Linux theme's sans list (Cantarell, Ubuntu, DejaVu Sans, system-ui, sans-serif) while the registry list has it (Cantarell, Ubuntu, Noto Sans, DejaVu Sans). On Omarchy the theme list would resolve DejaVu Sans if present while the registry resolves Noto Sans, so text is measured in one family and drawn in another. Adwaita Sans is in neither list, so a GNOME 48+ desktop gets Cantarell if it is still installed, otherwise the next entry.\n\nProposed shared order: Adwaita Sans, Cantarell, Ubuntu, Noto Sans, DejaVu Sans. A static list cannot follow a desktop that has several of these installed (a KDE box with Cantarell present would pick Cantarell). The stronger option is to ask fontconfig on Linux, fc-match sans-serif, which is what each desktop and Omarchy configure, and use the static list only when that fails. Decision wanted before the change.","files":null}]}